1
00:00:00.983 --> 00:00:03.750
This lesson is all about how structured
generation

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.000
with outlines works under the hood.

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.500
You'll learn how structured
generation works using an LLMs logits.

4
00:00:09.566 --> 00:00:13.433
Then you'll dive into the code
and see exactly how structured generation

5
00:00:13.433 --> 00:00:16.250
chooses
the next token. Let's have some fun.

6
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:19.583
Logit-based structure generation
is a much more efficient and flexible

7
00:00:19.583 --> 00:00:21.450
method for structuring your outputs.

8
00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.550
It's also known as constrained decoding.

9
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.700
Or it can be just called logit
based methods.

10
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.266
And recall, that what we're doing here,

11
00:00:27.266 --> 00:00:29.616
we talked about this
briefly in the first lesson.

12
00:00:29.616 --> 00:00:33.783
What we're doing is actually intercepting
the logits in the model and changing them

13
00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:36.533
to affect the probabilities
that come out of the model.

14
00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:40.216
Let's quickly refresh on many the benefits
of working with structured generation.

15
00:00:40.366 --> 00:00:42.500
It modifies the LLM outputs directly.

16
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.283
This means that you always get
the structure you defined.

17
00:00:45.283 --> 00:00:49.066
Recall that with proprietary models
using their JSON mode and re-prompting

18
00:00:49.066 --> 00:00:51.650
techniques
can sometimes fail and result

19
00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:53.483
you not getting back to the structure
you wanted.

20
00:00:53.483 --> 00:00:56.566
Another huge benefit,
is that the time cost during inference

21
00:00:56.566 --> 00:00:59.600
and working with structure
generation is basically zero.

22
00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.783
It is very, very lightweight
and you will not notice

23
00:01:02.783 --> 00:01:04.283
that you're using it during inference.

24
00:01:04.283 --> 00:01:06.950
You also get a much wider
range of structure.

25
00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.083
We'll talk about
this more in the next lesson,

26
00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:11.533
but you're not limited to strictly
working with JSON.

27
00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:13.116
Now, there is one slight catch

28
00:01:13.116 --> 00:01:15.333
when working with structure generation
to keep in mind,

29
00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:19.083
because we're working with the logits,
you need to have access to the logits.

30
00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:21.416
This means you're either
using an open weight model

31
00:01:21.416 --> 00:01:24.416
or you are in yourself
a proprietary model provider.

32
00:01:24.933 --> 00:01:27.366
Let's refresh on how LLMs generate text.

33
00:01:27.366 --> 00:01:29.183
You provide the LLM with a prompt.

34
00:01:29.183 --> 00:01:30.933
It's broken down into a of tokens.

35
00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:33.800
The tokens are fed in all at once
into the LLM.

36
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:37.483
The LLM then produces a bunch of weights
representing the relative

37
00:01:37.483 --> 00:01:39.950
probabilities of each possible next token.

38
00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:42.400
A token is sampled
and then appended to the prompt,

39
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.366
and this process is repeated until
we eventually reach the end of statement

40
00:01:46.366 --> 00:01:48.316
token terminating generation.

41
00:01:48.316 --> 00:01:51.216
Additionally,
you can set up a fixed token limit.

42
00:01:51.216 --> 00:01:54.950
Let's step through a real task
and see how an LLM would generate tokens.

43
00:01:55.166 --> 00:01:57.533
In this case,
we're using a visual language model

44
00:01:57.533 --> 00:02:00.366
and we're providing an image
along with the text prompt.

45
00:02:00.366 --> 00:02:02.000
Our prompt is asking the model to produce

46
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.483
either hot dog or not hot dog,
using only those labels.

47
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:10.483
Here is a feasible distribution of what
the initial token logits might look like

48
00:02:10.483 --> 00:02:15.483
for this prompt. We have H, ham, hamburger,
hot, hot dog, and not.

49
00:02:15.833 --> 00:02:18.500
These all make sense
because it is an image of a hamburger.

50
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.133
Even though we asked the model to respond
only with hot dog or not hot dog,

51
00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:24.050
it's quite possible
that the model is thinking

52
00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:26.650
that this is the hamburger
and so it wants the reply hamburger.

53
00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:30.366
After the logits are generated,
the token chosen is actually chosen

54
00:02:30.366 --> 00:02:32.466
by a sampler, not to logits themselves.

55
00:02:32.466 --> 00:02:35.250
There are many different sampling
strategies that can have different impacts

56
00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.566
on the quality of generation.
Here are two.

57
00:02:37.566 --> 00:02:39.300
The first one is a greedy sampler,

58
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.283
which simply always chooses
the highest probability token.

59
00:02:42.283 --> 00:02:44.650
In this case, it's choosing hamburger.

60
00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.050
In the other case,
we have a multinomial sampler

61
00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:48.183
which is going to choose from

62
00:02:48.183 --> 00:02:51.183
the logits proportion it is a
how strong the logits are.

63
00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:53.300
In this case, we might have chosen ham,

64
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.633
but of course we didn't get the structure
we wanted from that case.

65
00:02:55.633 --> 00:02:58.116
We wanted only hot dog or not hot dog.

66
00:02:58.116 --> 00:03:00.466
And we were going to choose
probably hamburger.

67
00:03:00.466 --> 00:03:00.883
So let's talk

68
00:03:00.883 --> 00:03:04.216
about how structure generation
works by modifying these logits directly.

69
00:03:04.316 --> 00:03:06.333
Here's the logits
we saw in the last slide.

70
00:03:06.333 --> 00:03:09.350
But we only want to use the labels
hot dog or not hot dog.

71
00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:11.950
So we're going to remove
ham and hamburger from these.

72
00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:15.916
Notice that H is still valid
because it's starting of the word hot dog.

73
00:03:16.183 --> 00:03:16.850
That's okay.

74
00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:20.383
So we're left with
H, hot, hot dog, and not. Now,

75
00:03:20.383 --> 00:03:22.516
we still use the same samples
we used before.

76
00:03:22.516 --> 00:03:25.900
We only change the logits themselves,
not the sampling process.

77
00:03:26.083 --> 00:03:29.233
So, again a greedy sampler might choose
not in this case.

78
00:03:29.233 --> 00:03:31.900
And the multinomial sampler
it might choose H.

79
00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:33.966
Now let's
go see how this works in practice.

80
00:03:35.066 --> 00:03:36.800
So, let's begin by pasting

81
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.216
some code in here that's going to ignore
some warnings that you might see here.

82
00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:44.133
Next, we're going to load
a small local model into memory.

83
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:49.216
In order to do so, all you have to do
is import outlines and then use outlines,

84
00:03:49.216 --> 00:03:53.666
dot models, dot transformers and pick
any language model from HuggingFace.

85
00:03:53.816 --> 00:03:55.350
In this case, we're going to use HuggingFace's

86
00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:59.433
SmolLM2
135 million parameter model.

87
00:03:59.616 --> 00:04:01.766
This is an extremely small model.

88
00:04:01.766 --> 00:04:02.883
We chose this mostly

89
00:04:02.883 --> 00:04:06.633
so it can fit on these notebooks,
but you can use any language model

90
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.416
as long as you have access
to the final output layer.

91
00:04:09.416 --> 00:04:12.016
As per usual
we begin by defining the structure

92
00:04:12.016 --> 00:04:13.750
we want our model to generate.

93
00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:18.066
In this case, we define a person
with fields, name, and age.

94
00:04:18.216 --> 00:04:21.583
Name may be any string, and age
may be any integer.

95
00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:25.133
Next, you're going to construct
a token generation function.

96
00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.600
A generator in outlines is a function

97
00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.600
that accepts any string prompt and returns
an object of the specified structure.

98
00:04:31.766 --> 00:04:35.333
You can see here
we're using outlines dot generate dot Json.

99
00:04:35.716 --> 00:04:38.216
We provide
the model that we loaded into memory.

100
00:04:38.216 --> 00:04:40.300
The object that we wish to receive.

101
00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.766
And then we're going
to set the sampler to greedy.

102
00:04:42.766 --> 00:04:45.366
So we take the most likely token
at every step.

103
00:04:45.366 --> 00:04:48.016
We're doing this mostly
so that every person in the course

104
00:04:48.016 --> 00:04:50.033
has access to the exact same results.

105
00:04:50.033 --> 00:04:52.383
Lastly, we're going to call track logits.

106
00:04:52.383 --> 00:04:56.066
Track logits is a utility function
that allows us to observe

107
00:04:56.066 --> 00:04:59.066
the token probabilities
at every step in the sequence.

108
00:04:59.266 --> 00:05:01.766
All right. Let's generate a random person.

109
00:05:01.766 --> 00:05:04.283
First, we need to cover
what's called prompt templating.

110
00:05:04.283 --> 00:05:07.133
So language models use specialized tokens

111
00:05:07.133 --> 00:05:10.633
to indicate the beginning of a system
prompt or the beginning of a user prompt.

112
00:05:10.966 --> 00:05:13.966
And the language model's response.

113
00:05:14.033 --> 00:05:18.233
Most SDKs handle the addition of these
tokens for you, but outlines does not.

114
00:05:18.566 --> 00:05:22.133
This is because managing your prompts
at a low level gives you more control

115
00:05:22.133 --> 00:05:23.816
over the generation process.

116
00:05:23.816 --> 00:05:26.816
So we've included this handy
utility function called template

117
00:05:27.083 --> 00:05:28.166
that accepts your model.

118
00:05:28.166 --> 00:05:31.166
It accepts a prompt
and an optional system prompt.

119
00:05:31.333 --> 00:05:33.583
When you apply chat templating,
you'll see this.

120
00:05:33.583 --> 00:05:36.916
This is the text
that usually goes to a language model.

121
00:05:37.166 --> 00:05:37.966
It's often hidden.

122
00:05:37.966 --> 00:05:41.066
We don't usually see the templated text
like this,

123
00:05:41.066 --> 00:05:44.066
but it's useful to know that this is
what's happening on the back end.

124
00:05:44.083 --> 00:05:46.833
So here is how you actually generate
a random person.

125
00:05:46.833 --> 00:05:48.300
We're going to take this prompt

126
00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.200
and pass it to the generator
that we just constructed.

127
00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.300
And then we're going to take a look
at the person.

128
00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:56.183
And it's worth noting here
that generator dot logits

129
00:05:56.183 --> 00:05:59.183
dot clear removes
any previously tracked logits.

130
00:05:59.183 --> 00:06:00.266
If you're going to call this function

131
00:06:00.266 --> 00:06:04.016
multiple times, make sure you clear
the logits tracker each time.

132
00:06:04.166 --> 00:06:06.800
As we can see here, we've got a person
named John.

133
00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.200
John is a 30 year old person.

134
00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.866
But how did we get the text
that allows us to have

135
00:06:12.983 --> 00:06:17.433
a person named John at age
30 that we can parse perfectly every time

136
00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:20.950
First,
let's take a look at the underlying JSON.

137
00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:23.083
Because person is a pydantic class.

138
00:06:23.083 --> 00:06:27.983
We can convert person into its underlying
JSON using pydantic model dump JSON.

139
00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:31.133
If you set indent equal to two,
you can kind of pretty print it.

140
00:06:31.133 --> 00:06:32.700
So it's nicer to look at.

141
00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:37.700
We know that the first part of this JSON
string has to be exactly this: curly brace

142
00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:42.883
quote, name, quote, colon,
beginning quote. Name has to be there

143
00:06:42.883 --> 00:06:45.950
because it's the very first field
in the person class

144
00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:49.233
that we specified the thing to
the right of that can be any string.

145
00:06:49.233 --> 00:06:50.883
So it has to be enclosed in quotes.

146
00:06:50.883 --> 00:06:53.433
And then we have to have an age
field again

147
00:06:53.433 --> 00:06:56.433
because it's in our person class
followed by an integer.

148
00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:00.700
So, let's take a look at the probability
of each token being generated.

149
00:07:00.866 --> 00:07:01.633
This code here

150
00:07:01.633 --> 00:07:05.683
will show the probabilities of each token
at the very first position.

151
00:07:05.716 --> 00:07:08.133
This is the first token
the model wants to generate.

152
00:07:08.133 --> 00:07:11.866
What you're seeing here is orange bars
representing the probability

153
00:07:11.866 --> 00:07:16.533
of selecting a token on the
y axis after outlines applies constraints.

154
00:07:16.666 --> 00:07:20.450
The blue bars here, indicate the probability
of the token being selected

155
00:07:20.666 --> 00:07:22.766
as if no constraints were applied.

156
00:07:22.766 --> 00:07:26.250
As you can see here,
the curly brace and curly brace quote,

157
00:07:26.483 --> 00:07:30.500
both of which are orange bars, have very
high probabilities under constraints

158
00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:35.183
90.8% probability for just the curly brace
and then curly brace

159
00:07:35.183 --> 00:07:40.183
quote has a 9.2% probability. Unconstrained,
the model wants to respond with here,

160
00:07:41.083 --> 00:07:42.366
I, and meet,

161
00:07:42.366 --> 00:07:46.716
neither of which are permissible under
the format that we specified in our Json.

162
00:07:46.883 --> 00:07:50.633
Structure generation in this case
is turning off the model's

163
00:07:50.633 --> 00:07:53.633
natural propensity
to respond in natural language.

164
00:07:53.633 --> 00:07:56.616
Here, I, and meet. You try this.

165
00:07:56.616 --> 00:08:01.116
So try changing the K argument
to show a different number of tokens.

166
00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.066
So if I set k equal to ten

167
00:08:03.066 --> 00:08:06.716
I can see the ten most probable tokens
that the model is considering.

168
00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:08.316
Again curly brace.

169
00:08:08.316 --> 00:08:11.016
Both of those are very high
probability under constraints.

170
00:08:11.016 --> 00:08:12.583
But the remaining tokens

171
00:08:12.583 --> 00:08:15.600
have a very high probability
without constraints being applied.

172
00:08:15.716 --> 00:08:18.716
All of which are natural language
responses.

173
00:08:18.983 --> 00:08:22.016
We know that the first field
name has to be name,

174
00:08:22.183 --> 00:08:24.650
and that the value of that name
can be any string.

175
00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:28.516
So when the model reaches the first field,
it has to choose tokens

176
00:08:28.516 --> 00:08:30.583
that combine to the string name.

177
00:08:30.583 --> 00:08:34.383
Let's look again at the tokens
available at position four.

178
00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:38.816
When we run this,
we'll see that the name token is by far

179
00:08:38.816 --> 00:08:43.166
the most likely under constraints
at 99.7% probability.

180
00:08:43.233 --> 00:08:44.216
Without constraints,

181
00:08:44.216 --> 00:08:48.133
the model may choose a user, name
with a capital N, person or user,

182
00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:51.000
but outlines has turned off
all of these tokens

183
00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:54.000
because they're not consistent
with the format that we expect.

184
00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:56.483
You'll note here that this is not 100%.

185
00:08:56.483 --> 00:08:59.483
There's a 0.3% probability somewhere else.

186
00:08:59.566 --> 00:09:04.566
And that's because it's allocated
to the remaining tokens N, N, A, and NAM,

187
00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:08.650
all of which can be combined
to make the phrase name.

188
00:09:08.833 --> 00:09:12.366
So let's take a look at the sequence
that we've generated so far.

189
00:09:12.516 --> 00:09:15.766
We can do this with generator dot logits
process dot sequence.

190
00:09:15.916 --> 00:09:19.400
And we can see that
we have curly brace, quote, name.

191
00:09:19.733 --> 00:09:22.850
After this has been sampled,
we know that the part following

192
00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:26.816
has to be quote colon quote
because that's valid JSON syntax.

193
00:09:26.966 --> 00:09:30.733
When we take a look
at the available tokens at this point

194
00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:34.950
we see that we have a very high
probability of anything that would combine

195
00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:39.950
to quote unquote, such as quote, colon,
just a quote unquote, colon quote, etc...

196
00:09:40.166 --> 00:09:41.133
You'll notice here

197
00:09:41.133 --> 00:09:44.850
that the constrained and unconstrained
probabilities are actually very close.

198
00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.050
And the reason is that by the time
the language model gets to this point,

199
00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:53.800
it knows that the thing following
should probably be quote colon,

200
00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.116
because most of the time your language
model has seen a lot of JSON.

201
00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:01.833
At this point,
we have the string like this.

202
00:10:01.833 --> 00:10:04.983
We now have an open quote,
which means that it's time for the model

203
00:10:04.983 --> 00:10:06.683
to start generating our name.

204
00:10:06.683 --> 00:10:10.550
So, hopefully the thing that gets picked
next looks something like a name.

205
00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:13.316
When we take a look
at the tokens at this position.

206
00:10:13.316 --> 00:10:17.833
We see that the model has a very high
probability of picking John 7%-ish probability

207
00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.116
E, A, L, and M.
John has a high enough probability.

208
00:10:22.383 --> 00:10:23.850
And we're using greedy sampling.

209
00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:25.433
So we'll just take John.

210
00:10:25.433 --> 00:10:29.400
You'll note here that the orange
and blue bars are essentially the same.

211
00:10:29.566 --> 00:10:33.366
That's because within a string field
such as the name field,

212
00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:37.800
the constrained and unconstrained
logits are roughly in agreement.

213
00:10:38.366 --> 00:10:41.933
We can also take a look
at how the model chooses to end

214
00:10:41.933 --> 00:10:45.266
the name field by looking at the tokens
in position seven here.

215
00:10:45.416 --> 00:10:46.950
And as we can see,

216
00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:50.750
the model has a very high percent
probability of choosing quote comma.

217
00:10:51.083 --> 00:10:55.250
This would stop the name field
and move on to the next field.

218
00:10:55.433 --> 00:10:58.433
There's a 26% probability of choosing Do,

219
00:10:58.433 --> 00:11:02.533
which would probably end up
constructing the name D.O.E,

220
00:11:02.683 --> 00:11:04.616
or John Doe.

221
00:11:04.616 --> 00:11:07.616
Let's also quickly
take a look at the age tokens.

222
00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:09.633
So you can see this here.

223
00:11:09.633 --> 00:11:13.250
In this case we're going to look
at the positions 9 and 12.

224
00:11:13.416 --> 00:11:14.550
When we print this out.

225
00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:19.466
We can see that age will be printed
with 100% probability under constraints.

226
00:11:19.466 --> 00:11:21.966
Because that's the field name
that we requested.

227
00:11:21.966 --> 00:11:25.983
And then we can see that the first token
under age to be generated

228
00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:27.516
is going to be a 3.

229
00:11:27.516 --> 00:11:31.266
We know that the one
if you add a 13 up here or change

230
00:11:31.266 --> 00:11:35.983
12 to a 13, you can see that
the next token will be a zero.

231
00:11:36.816 --> 00:11:39.600
After the name and age has been generated,
we know that

